<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0679">Remark <span data-tag="0679">9.4.1.8</span>.</a> Following the convention of Remark <a data-tag="03PU" href="/tag/03PU">4.9.0.4</a>, a regular cardinal $\kappa $ is small if it satisfies $\kappa &lt;  \Omega $, for some fixed strongly inaccessible cardinal $ \Omega $. In this case, an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $\kappa $-compactly generated (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0676" href="/tag/0676">9.4.1.5</a>) if and only if it is $(\kappa , \Omega )$-compactly generated (in the sense of Variant <a data-tag="0678" href="/tag/0678">9.4.1.7</a>). In particular, $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is compactly generated if and only if it is $(\aleph _0,  \Omega )$-compactly generated. See Remark <a data-tag="064P" href="/tag/064P">9.2.5.14</a>. </p>
